[07:29.02]Passage One
[07:30.75]Winter is a pain in the animal kingdom. 
[07:34.35]Birds can flee it by migrating to warmer regions but land beasts, including mammals, have no choice but to stick around. 
[07:43.72]To cope with it, many species have learned to spend the winter in a long sleep. 
[07:49.41]Some, like the Columbian ground squirrel, spend up to nine months of each year in their shelters.
[07:56.04] This conserves energy but leaves them with only three months to become well-rounded for the next winter and, crucially, to reproduce.
[08:05.55]To make matters worse, climate change is leading them to emerge from winter sleep later than usual. 
[08:12.36]Global warming means that creatures have longer ice-free periods to go about their evolutionary tasks. 
[08:20.12]But it can also disturb weather patterns, which may have the opposite effect.
[08:25.43]Because female squirrels remain in their place of birth, the researchers were able to tag and observe them and their offspring each year for the past two decades. 
[08:36.23]A typical female would bear three babies. 
[08:39.33]On average, only 30% of them survive the first winter, enough to sustain population numbers since female squirrels can expect three or four litters in their lifetime. 
[08:51.28]If the proportion falls, however, the population reduces.
[08:55.37]In the first decade of the study, the number of squirrels dropped just once. 
[09:00.28]But it fell in four of the past ten years. 
[09:03.68]Dr.Lane, an evolutionary biologist at the University of Edinburgh, speculates that this might be explained by the fact that over the past 20 years the late snow has delayed the melting of ice by half a day each year, on average, shortening the squirrels' breeding and feeding season by several days and disrupting their life cycles.
[09:29.19]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[09:34.87]9. How do many land animals spend the winter according to the passage?
[09:54.92]10. What does global warming mean to animals?
[10:12.70]11. What might explain the reduced squirrel population according to Dr. Lane?
[10:32.69]Passage Two
[10:34.74]Lionfish are known for their beauty and unusual look. 
[10:39.38]But lionfish are also poisonous, with extremely long and separated spines to carry and shoot poison. 
[10:47.54]The native environment for lionfish includes the South Pacific and Indian Ocean. 
[10:53.63]However, sea experts say lionfish were at some time released into the Atlantic Ocean, likely by people who had kept them in tanks. 
[11:03.29]Lionfish are currently considered a great threat to coral reefs and related sea life in coastal waters of the southeastern United States and Caribbean. 
[11:13.88]Scientists say the fish is a top predator that competes for food and space with native fish in the area.
[11:22.00]Now there is a new solution for fighting back. 
[11:25.26]University researchers are developing an underwater robot to hunt down, kill and collect lionfish. 
[11:32.57]A student-led team at Worcester Polytechnic Institute in Massachusetts developed the robot. 
[11:39.30]The team recently demonstrated the device and is continuing to test and make changes to it. 
[11:45.24]The team says that the robot is designed to recognize the lionfish, chase it and shoot the animal with a spear, which then separates from the robot and floats the fish to the surface for collection. 
[11:57.67]Craig Putnam is a computer science professor at Worcester Polytechnic Institute and a director of the school¡¯s Robotics Engineering Program. 
[12:07.49]He says the robot was built to simplify the process of hunting down and collecting this aggressive fish. 
[12:14.43]Putnam said his team trained the robot system by showing it thousands of images of lionfish in different colors, from many directions and under different lighting conditions. 
[12:25.45]The robot can correctly identify lionfish about 95% of the time, he added.
[12:32.22]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[12:38.44]12. What does the speaker say about lionfish?
[12:55.81]13. What are lionfish doing to the Atlantic Ocean?
[13:14.53]14. What can we learn about the team that developed the robot?
[13:33.03]15. How did the team train the robot system to hunt down the lionfish?
